References: <483C636F.5080508@yale.edu>
Message-ID: <839691710805271251y5a51a7ccl6a2ec1e28730a842@mail.gmail.com>

Hi Steve,

Have you upgraded to the newest version of Amelia? We recently
released a version that fixed this bug. You can upgrade to the newest
version by first making sure you have the most recent version of R
(2.7.0) and then run the following command:

install.packages("Amelia", repos="http://gking.harvard.edu")

Let us know if this does not solve your problem.

In response to your last question, you can assign the amelia output to
an R object like so:

amelia.out <- amelia(jan, m=5, p2s=2, empri=0, tolerance=0.0001,
startvals=1, archive=TRUE, incheck=T, ords=c("v1", "v2", "v3", "v4",
"v5", "v7", "vv1", "vv2", "vv3", "vv4", "vv5", "vv7"), collect=FALSE,
outname="jan", write.out=TRUE, keep.data=TRUE)

which will stop R from dumping the amelia output to the screen. You
can also access the imputed datasets in this object as amelia.out[[i]]
will be the ith imputed dataset.


hope that helps,
matt.

